//! Claims files: a plain-text way to write a DAG of claims.
//!
//! One row per piece of evidence, six tab-separated columns:
//!
//!   label  rests_on  witness  statement  path  quote
//!
//! - `label` names the claim within this file (e.g. `C3`). Rows sharing a
//!   label are one claim; their statement and witness must match.
//! - `rests_on` is `-` or a comma-separated list of labels in this file:
//!   the claims this one is built from.
//! - `path` is a repository file, ingested as an artifact; `quote` must occur
//!   in it exactly once (`\t`, `\n`, `\\` are unescaped first). Both are `-`
//!   for a row that adds no evidence. The path must be relative, made of
//!   plain components only (no `..`, `.`, empty or absolute parts), contain
//!   no symlink, and resolve inside the repository: otherwise a claims file
//!   could pull `/etc/hosts` or `~/.ssh/id_ed25519` into the graph, and the
//!   quote check would become an oracle for guessing their contents.
//!
//! Lines that are blank or start with `#` are ignored.
//!
//! Problems are collected per row, not raised at the first one: a proposal
//! from an LLM is expected to contain errors, and the point is to see all of
//! them. A claim whose evidence all failed, or that rests on a failed claim,
//! fails too.

